1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,025
No public IPv4. No NAT. No internet gateway.

2
00:00:04,275 --> 00:00:07,075
Yet this private EC2 instance can reach S3.

3
00:00:07,555 --> 00:00:11,942
First, DNS resolves the S3 name to S3 service
IP addresses.

4
00:00:12,192 --> 00:00:14,292
The subnet route table looks for a match.

5
00:00:14,542 --> 00:00:18,992
The destination matches the regional, AWS-
managed S3 prefix list.

6
00:00:19,242 --> 00:00:21,618
That route targets a gateway endpoint.

7
00:00:22,098 --> 00:00:26,360
Follow the packet. Same-Region S3. Entirely on
the AWS network.

8
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:29,760
No interface ENI. No private endpoint IP. No
NAT.

9
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:32,002
But a path is not permission.

10
00:00:32,252 --> 00:00:35,790
Security groups and network ACLs must allow the
traffic.

11
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:40,790
IAM, the endpoint policy, and the bucket policy
still govern object access.

12
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:44,145
The route changed. The permissions didn't
disappear.
